# TODO - Native PCI

## Objective

Complete source for exact-device identification, PCI ownership and power D0,
BAR/MMIO access, concurrency, command rollback and safe teardown, based on
documented APIs and public source. **No physical PCI reads.**

## Acceptance

- [ ] Implement/review the stated source and integrate on main.
- [ ] Define exclusive PCI ownership, D0, BAR/MMIO and rollback in source.
- [ ] Ground decisions in public code, published standards and SDK/KDK references.
- [ ] Record unresolved assumptions and fail-closed behavior.
- [ ] Complete with source/static evidence, NEVER requiring a physical Dell test.

## Required source deliverables

- [x] Implement the controller that retains/opens the actual PCI provider,
      verifies physical 168c:0036 without a 0030 spoof, and supplies genuine
      serialized D0/ownership epochs; wire it into start/stop and power events.
- [x] Map the provider's assigned BAR with checked bounds, ordered LE MMIO
      and device posted-write completion. Use command-width-safe writes;
      never size a live BAR or invent a mapping/ownership success.
- [x] Implement sleep, provider termination, partial-start failure and resume:
      revoke callbacks before unmapping/closing, track command rollback and
      refuse access after owner/power loss. State which context may block.
- [x] Connect read-only inspection and active register capabilities to their
      real callers while preserving TX/DMA/interrupt safety gates. A backend
      contract or permanently disabled source branch is not this integration.

## Start here and next implementation step

`src/SimpleATH.cpp` now owns the real IOPCIDevice through a work loop and
command gate. Its normal target path opens PCI, verifies live identity and
PMCSR D0, maps BAR0, decodes SREV, quiesces the MAC, executes RTC bootstrap
and acquires the NVM board lease. Sleep stops the session before the provider
powers down; wake starts a fresh monotonically increasing epoch. Provider
termination revokes access without attempting hardware restoration.

`Ar9565NativePci` provides separate active and inspection capabilities; the
three-register inspection allowlist stays bounded. Active writes use a
same-device SREV MMIO read to drain posted writes. No experimental compile
flag excludes either PCI or DMA from the normal Xcode target, and Info.plist
now declares IOPCIFamily 2.9. Matching tolerates the existing 0030 registry spoof; the
live configuration identity must still be physical 168c:0036.

Startup reset/NVM calls execute synchronously in sleepable start/PM thread
context while holding the gate. No worker or DMA allocation waits to reenter
that gate. Native DMA packet allocation still requires a separate client
thread when the hardware queue owner is implemented; do not invoke its
blocking preparation from this gate.

Remaining: integrate calibrated RF/channel setup and the DMA/IRQ owner with
this lifecycle, then connect IO80211. Those are required implementations,
not evidence that the whole Wi-Fi task is complete. macOS binary linkage,
exact-build virtual dispatch and physical operation remain to be tested.

Primary references: [IOPCIDevice](https://github.com/apple-oss-distributions/IOPCIFamily/blob/main/IOPCIDevice.cpp)
and [IOService contract](https://github.com/apple-oss-distributions/xnu/blob/main/iokit/IOKit/IOService.h).
Useful host-fake regressions: failed command enable/readback, unrelated command
bit drift, D0 loss between callbacks, double stop and stale-epoch access.
These cases do not prove that actual IOPCIFamily or PCI hardware behaves correctly.

## Evidence

- Source commits, reference URLs/version/rights, static review and lint diagnostics.
- Theoretical completeness only; no kext build, device scan, packet logs or hardware claims.
- Add offline host tests only where material behavior/error paths warrant them;
  run through tools/validate.py --tests without treating them as hardware proof.
